Publish a package to the registry

Usage: pnpm publish [OPTIONS] [PACKAGE]

Arguments:
  [PACKAGE]
          Tarball or directory to publish. Defaults to the current directory

Options:
      --dry-run
          Do everything `publish` would do except uploading to the registry

      --ignore-scripts
          Don't run publish-related lifecycle scripts

      --force
          Publish even if the version is already in the registry

      --batch
          Send all workspace packages in a single request (requires `--recursive`)

      --tag <TAG>
          Register the published package under this tag instead of `latest`

      --access <ACCESS>
          Publish the package as `public` or `restricted`
          
          [possible values: public, restricted]

      --provenance
          Generate a provenance attestation for the published package

      --otp <OTP>
          One-time password for two-factor-authenticated registries

      --publish-wait-timeout <MILLISECONDS>
          Wait up to this many milliseconds per package for registry availability. A batch upload uses one timeout per registry group. Zero disables waiting (default)

      --embed-readme
          Embed the README contents in the published manifest

      --skip-manifest-obfuscation
          Keep the original `packageManager` field and publish-lifecycle scripts in the published manifest instead of stripping them

      --release-notes <FILE>
          A file of release notes to send to a registry declared with `serverType: pnpr`. Publishing one package, they are its version notes. Publishing several with `--batch`, they are the notes of the whole release

      --no-release-notes
          Send no release notes, not even those read from `CHANGELOG.md` or change intents

      --publication-name <NAME>
          The name of the release that `--batch` publishes to a registry declared with `serverType: pnpr`, such as `v12.0.0`

      --publish-branch <PUBLISH_BRANCH>
          The branch publishing is allowed from. Overrides the `publishBranch` setting. Without either, defaults to `master` / `main`

      --no-git-checks
          Skip the git working-tree / branch / remote checks

      --json
          Print the per-package publish summary in JSON

      --report-summary
          Recursive only: write a `pnpm-publish-summary.json` report listing the packages that were published

      --no-variants
          Publish only the archive that targets `any` of a package that declares `publishConfig.variants`, such as to a registry that does not serve platform variants

      --upload-variant <TARGET>
          Pack the archive of the `publishConfig.variants` entry this target names, such as `linux-x64-musl` or `any`, upload it to the registry's staging area, and print `<target>=<integrity>`. Publishes nothing. Can be repeated

      --variant-integrity <TARGET=INTEGRITY>
          Publish the archive another job uploaded with `--upload-variant`, as `<target>=<integrity>`, or `<name>~<target>=<integrity>` with `--recursive`. Repeated once per archive

  -y, --yes
          Automatically answer yes to prompts

      --env-file <PATH>
          Load environment variables from a dotenv-style file before running. Repeat to layer more files: later files override earlier files, and variables already in the environment are never overridden. Relative paths resolve against the invocation working directory. The loaded variables are visible to config resolution (`PNPM_CONFIG_<KEY>` overrides and `${VAR}` tokens in user-level `.npmrc` files), to lifecycle scripts, and to any pnpm the command dispatches to

  -C, --dir <DIR>
          Set working directory. Accepted anywhere on the command line, before or after the subcommand, like every other rc-option
          
          [default: .]

      --store-dir <DIR>
          Directory in which the package store is created. Relative paths are resolved from the workspace root, or from `--dir` outside a workspace

      --state-dir <DIR>
          Directory in which pnpm persists machine-local state

      --npmrc-auth-file <NPMRC_AUTH_FILE>
          Path to an `.npmrc` to read auth settings from, overriding the default `~/.npmrc`
          
          [alias: --userconfig]

      --ignore-workspace
          Run as if the project were standalone, ignoring any `pnpm-workspace.yaml` above it

      --workspace-packages <WORKSPACE_PACKAGES>
          Glob patterns selecting the workspace's projects, overriding the `packages` field of `pnpm-workspace.yaml`. Repeat to add more

      --registry <REGISTRY>
          Base URL of the npm registry to resolve and fetch packages from. Universal rc-option: accepted on every command and layered onto the config like `--config.registry=<url>`. Commands that expose their own `--registry` still read the same value

      --https-proxy <HTTPS_PROXY>
          Proxy for HTTPS registry and tarball requests

      --http-proxy <HTTP_PROXY>
          Proxy for HTTP registry and tarball requests

      --no-proxy <NO_PROXY>
          Hosts that bypass configured proxies

      --color[=<COLOR>]
          Force colored output

      --reporter <REPORTER>
          Reporter output format

          Possible values:
          - default:     Rich visual output: a progress line, a packages diff, lifecycle output, and a `Done in ...` summary. Renders in place on a terminal and falls back to `append-only` output when stdout is not a terminal
          - append-only: Like `default` but forces the append-only rendering even on a TTY — one line per update, no cursor movement
          - ndjson:      Newline-delimited JSON on stderr
          - silent:      No progress output

      --loglevel <LOGLEVEL>
          What level of logs to print. `silent` selects the silent reporter over any `--reporter` choice; the other levels cap the default reporter's output
          
          [possible values: silent, error, warn, info, debug]

      --no-progress
          Disable dependency and download progress output

      --stream
          Stream a recursive command's script output as it arrives, one prefixed line at a time

      --aggregate-output
          Hold each script's streamed output until the script exits, then print it as one block

      --use-stderr
          Divert the reporter's output to stderr, leaving stdout for the command's own result

  -r, --recursive
          Run the command for every project in the workspace instead of only the project in `--dir`

  -F, --filter <FILTER>
          Select which workspace projects to run on. Repeat to add more. Each selector can be a name pattern (`@scope/*`), a path (`./pkg`), a dependency query (`foo...`), an exclusion (`!bar`), a directory (`{dir}`), or a changed-since query (`[since]`)

      --filter-prod <FILTER_PROD>
          Like `--filter`, but follow only production dependencies when selecting projects

  -w, --workspace-root
          Run the command on the root workspace project

      --fail-if-no-match
          Exit with code 1 when the `--filter` / `--filter-prod` selectors match no workspace project

      --no-fail-if-no-match
          Let a command run over an empty selection, overriding a `failIfNoMatch: true` setting

      --include-workspace-root
          Also run a recursive command on the root workspace project, which `run` / `exec` / `add` / `test` otherwise leave out

      --no-include-workspace-root
          Leave the root workspace project out of a recursive command, overriding an `includeWorkspaceRoot: true` setting

      --test-pattern <TEST_PATTERN>
          Glob patterns naming test files, used by the `[since]` `--filter` selector to decide which changes count

      --changed-files-ignore-pattern <CHANGED_FILES_IGNORE_PATTERN>
          Glob patterns of changed files that the `[since]` `--filter` selector should ignore

      --sort
          Keep recursive workspace projects sorted topologically

      --no-sort
          Run recursive workspace projects in workspace order

      --reverse
          Process recursive workspace projects in reverse order

      --workspace-concurrency <workspace_concurrency>
          Maximum number of workspace projects to process in parallel, or `Infinity` for no limit

      --parallel
          Run scripts in every selected workspace project concurrently, disregarding topological sorting

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
